00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:35,760
and has to wait, Alice gives Bob the LSR private key, Bob gives Alice the BobR private key,

44
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:38,760
they broadcast, the counterpart takes all the funds.

45
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:40,400
So what are some optimizations here?

46
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:44,400
The basic idea of the script is, okay, key A can, you know, you can have a signature

47
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:50,760
from key A and you have to wait a day or two or five or whatever, or you can have a signature

48
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:55,800
from key B and a signature from key C.

49
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:57,680
So what are some optimizations here?

50
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:03,520
The revealing part, any way to make that smaller, more efficient?

51
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:09,920
So one idea is either pre-image instead of a signature, right?

52
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:13,480
You're just revealing something, you're not actually committing to any data.

53
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:18,120
So Bob could, when Bob reveals his secret, he could just be, here's a pre-image, and

54
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:22,360
you can get, instead of like 70 bytes for a signature, you get down to like 20 bytes

55
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:26,080
for a hash and a pre-image, right?

56
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,920
Because, so how would it work?

57
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:36,080
You just say, okay, Alice signs if Alice knows BobR's pre-image, right?

58
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:39,640
The BobR, BobR doesn't have to actually sign.

59
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,280
You can just know it.

60
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:43,920
That would work.

61
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:48,480
So actually that was sort of the initial idea, I think that's what's written in the paper.

62
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:51,280
But you can do it even smaller.

63
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:55,640
So an even smaller way to do it is combine keys.

64
00:04:55,640 --> 00:05:00,360
So in the elliptic curve that we're using, in, you know, all these groups, all these

65
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:04,800
signature schemes, if you add two keys together, you can get a third key.

66
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:09,960
And it's using the addition operation that's defined on this group.

67
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:14,440
So in the case where you have, you know, key B and key C, and you add them together to

68
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:20,240
get, say, key R, what's the private key for key R?

69
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:22,640
Anyone?

70
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:28,520
You've got key B, private key, key C, which has a private key.

71
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:31,040
You add the two public keys up together.

72
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:36,600
Yeah, it's the sum of the private keys, right?

73
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:44,840
So public key B is just private key B times the generator point G, plus C times G equals

74
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:50,920
R times G. And then you can just, you know, move the parentheses around.

75
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:57,960
So B plus C, the private keys added together times the generator point is still the same.

76
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:59,960
So this is a really nice property.

77
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,240
It's sort of the main property that we use for all signatures and all this stuff.

78
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:11,760
But what that means is you can say, here, you know, I take your public key.

79
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:14,680
I'm going to compute, you know, come up with my own private key, compute the public key

80
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:21,360
for that, add them together and say, hey, we've got this new shared public key.

81
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:26,240
And neither of us can sign with it yet because we don't know each other's private keys.

82
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:30,280
But if either of us reveals the private key, then the other party can sign with it, right?

83
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:37,640
So if this is Bob's and this is Alice's, they can both compute little R times G by doing

84
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:38,640
this operation, right?

85
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:42,880
They both share public keys and compute the shared public key.

86
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:50,680
But they cannot sign because neither of them knows little R. And then let's say Alice says,

87
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:54,000
OK, Bob, here's this little C, gives it to Bob.

88
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,320
Bob says, OK, now I know little R. But Alice doesn't, right?

89
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:05,320
So this is a really nice way to do it because it saves a lot of space.

90
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:12,080
So instead, the script is now just key D, oh, I should have put R, oops, key D or key

91
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:14,120
A and time, right?

92
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:20,600
So instead of having this key and time passes or these other two keys both sign, you just

93
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:26,600
have a single key that can sign immediately or another key that can sign after a period

94
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:28,360
of time.

95
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:32,360
So the actual script used is this.

96
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:40,800
You say op if, the key R, op else, delay, object sequence verify, optrop, then the key

97
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:42,440
A, then the end if, then the object sig.

98
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:45,720
So I'll walk through this because it's a little confusing.

99
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:50,880
The op if takes an item off the stack and it's either true or false.

100
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:54,560
So the first thing you have to do is you have to indicate which of these two paths you're

101
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:56,480
going to use, right?

102
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:02,560
Either you're going to put key R on the stack or you're going to put this delay number, which

103
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:07,840
is say 100 blocks, put an object sequence verify to verify that it has been 100 blocks

104
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:11,400
since you created the transaction you're spending.

105
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:13,440
And then you have to drop the delay.

106
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:16,640
This was to ensure compatibility since it's a software.

107
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:21,720
So old nodes would see this, old nodes would see this, they would see a no op and then

108
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:25,600
they would see the drop to get rid of this whole thing.

109
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:28,520
So the old nodes wouldn't see it at all.

110
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:32,360
Then you have a key A put on the stack and then you end the if statement and then you

111
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:33,560
check signatures.

112
00:08:33,560 --> 00:08:41,480
So really the if statement is just determining which key gets pushed onto the stack.

113
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:46,680
After the if statement there's a check sig to check the signature from that key.

114
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:52,960
So for example, if you're using the revocation key, the key that has been combined and revealed

115
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:58,080
to you, you just put a 1 on the stack and then a signature from R.

116
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:03,560
And the op if resolves the true so it executes this by just pushing the key onto the stack.

117
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:09,080
Then it executes op else, skips to op end if and then does op check sig.

118
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:14,680
So basically you say 1, execute op if, key R, skip to check sig.

119
00:09:14,680 --> 00:09:18,080
So it's just you need to sign with this key.

120
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:20,800
Or you say 0 and signature for May.

121
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:29,000
And then so the op if takes a 0, it skips to op else because it's not true.

122
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:36,200
It performs pushes delay onto the stack, performs check sequence verify to make sure that enough

123
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:41,280
time has passed, drops the delay number off the stack, which is a little ugly.

124
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:46,520
It would be nicer if op check sequence verified did that itself, but it doesn't.

125
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:54,160
Then pushes key A onto the stack, ends the if statement and checks the signature.

126
00:09:54,160 --> 00:10:01,040
So it's a little more complicated than in C where you just can say key D or key A and

127
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:01,800
time.

128
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:08,280
But this is stack based and it's not too hard when you get these things to work.

129
00:10:08,280 --> 00:10:12,680
So any questions about this setup?

130
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:13,440
Yes.

131
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:15,720
So what's op drop again?

132
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:19,120
Op drop takes the top most item on the stack and deletes it.

133
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:23,400
And all the lower items come back up.

134
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:31,000
Yeah, it's just so that compatibility wise, there would be ways to,

135
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,240
since op check sequence verify, basically this used to be a no op where it did nothing.

136
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:39,880
And then the soft fork was, OK, we're redefining no op three, I believe,

137
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:46,680
to op check sequence verify where it looks at basically the age of your inputs.

138
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:51,440
And how many confirmations your inputs have had or really the specific input you're spending

139
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,680
in this case.

140
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:55,640
Old nodes would just see it as a no op.

141
00:10:55,640 --> 00:11:01,280
And so you could put something like delay op check in sequence verify and then an op

142
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:07,200
code around here that says, make sure the top item on the stack is greater than five.

143
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:13,160
And if this consumed the delay value by taking it off the stack, the old nodes might say,

144
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:14,120
oh, this is invalid.

145
00:11:14,120 --> 00:11:21,120
Even though this is a no op, the subsequent op code causes this to fail.

146
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:25,040
Whereas in the new version, it would be like, no, this is good because this consumes that

147
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:26,600
value.

148
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:30,200
And that would actually make it a hard fork because then there would be transactions

149
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:35,960
where the old nodes thought it was not good and the new nodes thought, yes, this is OK.

150
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:45,080
So to ensure that this only restricts the validity of scripts, it doesn't pop that off.

151
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:47,080
So you have to manually drop it.

152
00:11:47,080 --> 00:11:48,520
And drop is there from before.

153
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:52,760
So to an old node, it would just say delay, no op drop.

154
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:55,400
And it's like, OK, you put something on the stack, you do nothing, and then you take it

155
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:56,400
off the stack.

156
00:11:56,400 --> 00:11:57,400
Fine.

157
00:11:57,400 --> 00:11:58,400
Yeah.

158
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:01,040
So that's just a compatibility thing.

159
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:04,760
If you were going to make it from scratch, you'd probably do it differently.

160
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:06,680
Other questions about this?

161
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:08,960
OK, cool.

162
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:14,840
So OK, so then you reveal keys.

163
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:21,280
You keep revealing things each time you make a new state.

164
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:24,600
And you can do these several times a second.

165
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:26,440
You can do it 10 times a second, something like that.

166
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:29,760
So you can have millions, billions of different old transactions.

167
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:35,560
The problem is you do have to remember all of these old private keys that your counterparty

168
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:36,560
has given to you.

169
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:42,240
Because if you forget one, they might broadcast a transaction where you really need to know

170
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:45,400
that private key in order to grab the money immediately.

171
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:53,000
So if you, for example, you forget, this was revoked, but you forgot about it.

172
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,200
And you're Alice.

173
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:55,880
And then Bob broadcasts this.

174
00:12:55,880 --> 00:13:01,720
You really need to know that private key so you can grab Bob's five coins with your combined

175
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:02,720
key.

176
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:06,800
So you need to keep track of all of these old things.

177
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:08,320
So it's 32 bytes each.

178
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:10,160
It's not great for scalability.

179
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,800
So there's actually a couple of ways you could do this.

180
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:19,280
How can you remember a chain of 32 byte secrets?

181
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:25,480
A Merkle tree itself would have a root here, and then a bunch of little hashes that go

182
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:30,080
up to the tree, the tree's root.

183
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:33,560
So if these were secrets, you can't go back down, right?

184
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:39,240
So if you're like, OK, I got secret 0, secret 1, secret 2, secret 3, well I've got the

185
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:43,280
parents of these two things, but I can't actually remember them any better.

186
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:48,520
So the simple, like a real simple way, say, OK, I've got, and you can construct it however

187
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:49,520
you want, right?

188
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:53,640
Obviously, if your counterpart is not cooperating and these secret numbers are just completely

189
00:13:53,640 --> 00:13:55,960
random, there's nothing you can do, right?

190
00:13:55,960 --> 00:14:01,560
If you just say, hey, I'm going to give you secret 0, secret 1, secret 2, there's no special

191
00:14:01,560 --> 00:14:04,320
properties about these things, I can't do anything.

192
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:08,960
The simplest way, what's a real simple way to say, OK, I'm going to reveal secrets sequentially

193
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:19,920
and then you can store fewer of them.

194
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:23,800
It's sort of like a blockchain, right?

195
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:35,520
So you could just say, OK, well, I create secret 3, secret 2 is the hash of secret 3,

196
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:40,000
secret 1 is the hash of secret 2.

197
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,200
And so now I reveal 1, you don't know what 2 is, you know, and so on.

198
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:51,960
So 0 equals hash of 1, so you can just have a linear chain like this, right?

199
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:54,360
So 0, 1, 2, 3.

200
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:57,920
If you remember 3, you can compute 2 and 1 and 0.

201
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:01,400
If you remember 2, you can compute 1 and 0, but not 3.

202
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:02,400
So you can do that.

203
00:15:03,160 --> 00:15:07,440
That would actually probably work fine, but we always want to make unnecessary complex

204
00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:09,880
optimizations to things.

205
00:15:09,880 --> 00:15:14,560
So the problem with this is, if you've got a billion of them and you need to go back

206
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:20,320
to the first one, you need to compute a billion hash operations.

207
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:21,520
That's kind of slow.

208
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:27,800
So what we do or what the basic idea, there's like multiple implementations, slight variations.

209
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:29,080
You have a hash tree.

210
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:33,680
So you reveal secrets one at a time, you store only log n secrets, and you recompute any

211
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:38,960
secret you want with something around log n operations.

212
00:15:38,960 --> 00:15:40,000
I called it Elkrim.

213
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:44,040
I think there's an actual paper that basically is this called GGM.

214
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:48,080
It's like, I think written by people at MIT.

215
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:51,760
But I called it Elkrim because it's basically Merkle Tree backwards because the arrows point

216
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:54,640
the other direction.

217
00:15:54,680 --> 00:16:02,760
So in this case, if you start with a root, you start with something up here, and you

218
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:09,920
say, OK, if I want to descend to the left, I append a 0 to the hash value of this root,

219
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:11,640
and I hash to get down.

220
00:16:11,640 --> 00:16:17,120
And if I want to go right, I append a 1 at the end, hash that, and then I go down here.

221
00:16:17,120 --> 00:16:21,040
So what that lets you do is, there's a sender and a receiver.

222
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:24,880
The sender just computes a root and stores that.

223
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:27,400
They make a random root up, store it.

224
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:31,080
And then they also keep track of, OK, how many secrets have I given?

225
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:37,280
The receiver just receives the first one, number 0, can't do anything.

226
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:41,080
